WEBVTT

00:00:00.132 --> 00:00:04.675
Spoofing may present differently from simple signal loss because the receiver can report a

00:00:04.675 --> 00:00:06.560
position or time that appears valid.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.840
Warning signs are therefore inconsistencies among the map, visual scene, compass, route,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.035
telemetry, and expected movement.

00:00:13.035 --> 00:00:15.850
Examples include an apparent jump while the aircraft is visibly stable,

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.360
impossible speed or location, a map track that leaves the observed route,

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.810
an unexpected home point, or navigation commands that do not fit the crew's view.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:26.650
Compass and G N S S disagreement may indicate several problems,

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:31.340
including magnetic interference, calibration issues, sensor faults, reflected signals,

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:32.660
or a navigation anomaly.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.520
Treat the mismatch as reduced confidence, not immediate proof of spoofing.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.575
Telemetry inconsistency matters over time.

00:00:38.575 --> 00:00:42.960
Compare commanded movement, groundspeed, direction, altitude source, visual references,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.360
and logged position.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.890
Preserve screenshots or notes only when doing so does not distract from safe control.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.810
Independent time or location references can strengthen review after landing.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.280
They should come from trusted systems with known provenance,

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.880
not from another display that quietly depends on the same receiver or data source.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.075
Confirmation is difficult from one receiver and one symptom.

00:01:01.075 --> 00:01:05.140
High-confidence attribution may require specialized equipment, independent receivers,

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.900
broader reports, technical expertise,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.960
and regulatory investigation outside the pilot's role.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.200
Operationally, distrust position-dependent automation when cues conflict.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.690
Simplify the mission, maintain visual control,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:19.560
use trained manual or degraded-mode procedures, move toward a safe landing area,

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.190
and report the incident without overstating the cause.
